---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { readFileSync, existsSync } from 'node:fs';
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';
const resultsDir = fileURLToPath(new URL('../../../../app/eval/results/', import.meta.url));
const read = (name: string) => (existsSync(resultsDir + name) ? JSON.parse(readFileSync(resultsDir + name, 'utf8')) : null);
const fixture = read('fixture.json');
const model = read('claude.json');
const pct = (x: number | null) => (x == null ? 'n/a' : Math.round(x * 100) + ' %');
const ci = (m: any) => (!m || m.rate == null ? 'n/a' : `${(100 * m.rate).toFixed(1)} % (95 % CI ${(100 * m.lo).toFixed(1)}–${(100 * m.hi).toFixed(1)} %, ${m.k} of ${m.n})`);
const fmt = (iso: string) => new Date(iso).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric' });
const runs = [{ label: 'Validators on prepared records', note: 'Proves the checks themselves: a record with an invented drug, dose or diagnosis must be blocked. Offline, no model.', r: fixture }, ...(model ? [{ label: 'Model output on the same cases', note: 'The model structures the raw notes; the same checks then run on its output.', r: model }] : [])];
---
<Base title="Quality" description="How the AI Veterinary documentation assistant is tested, with the current numbers. Published with every release.">
  <article class="wrap narrow hero prose">
    <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/vets">For veterinary teams</a></p>
    <h1>Quality, with numbers</h1>
    <p class="lead">Every tool in this market claims accuracy; almost none says how it is measured. This page is our answer: the test set, the rules, and the current results, regenerated from the actual test run with every release.</p>

    <h2>What we measure</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Measure</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Release rule</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Blocking findings</td><td>Anything in the record that is not in the vet's notes: a drug, a dose, a number, a diagnosis, or an item with no source</td><td><strong>0</strong> on model output, or no release</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Drug mismatches</td><td>A drug name in the record that the vet did not write</td><td><strong>0</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Unsourced items</td><td>Facts without a valid pointer into the notes</td><td><strong>0</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Missing-information recall</td><td>Of the gaps a vet would want flagged (drug without dose, test without result…), how many we flag</td><td>≥ 95 %</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Must-fail case</td><td>A deliberately wrong record (invented drug, dose and diagnosis) that the checks must block</td><td>Always blocked</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>Rules can only get stricter: a release may raise a floor, never lower one.</p>

    <h2>How the rates are defined</h2>
    <p>Each rate comes with a 95 % confidence interval (Wilson), because with a few dozen cases a bare percentage claims more than is known. The definitions are in <code>app/src/metrics.ts</code>, so anyone can recompute them from the published results.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Source attribution</strong>: facts in the record that point to text in the vet's notes and raised no blocking finding, out of all facts. One fact is one complaint, history item, vital sign, exam finding, test, assessment, differential, treatment, medication, follow-up or instruction.</li>
      <li><strong>Unsupported content</strong>: facts with a blocking finding (a drug, dose or number the vet did not write, or a source pointing outside the notes), out of all facts.</li>
      <li><strong>Cases with a blocking finding</strong>: cases where the vet would be stopped at least once before approval.</li>
      <li><strong>Missing-field detection</strong>: gaps the case expects to be flagged (a drug without a dose, a test without a result, a vaccine without a batch) that were flagged.</li>
      <li><strong>Missing-flag precision</strong>: flags raised that the case actually expects, out of all flags raised. A wrong "missing" flag costs the vet a click and makes the real ones easier to ignore.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The deliberately wrong must-fail case is left out of the rates; it only proves the checks work.</p>

    <h2>Current results</h2>
    {runs.map(({ label, note, r }) => (
      <div class="card" style="margin:16px 0">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0">{label}</h3>
        <p class="pp-hint">{note}</p>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Run</th><td>{fmt(r.run_at)} · prompt <code>{r.prompt}</code> · languages {r.languages.join(', ').toUpperCase()}</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Cases</th><td>{r.cases} ({r.failed} failed)</td></tr>
          {r.metrics && <>
            <tr><th>Source attribution</th><td>{ci(r.metrics.attribution)}</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Unsupported content</th><td>{ci(r.metrics.unsupported_content)}</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Cases with a blocking finding</th><td>{ci(r.metrics.cases_with_blocking_finding)}</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Missing-field detection</th><td>{ci(r.metrics.missing_field_detection)}</td></tr>
            {r.metrics.missing_flag_precision && <tr><th>Missing-flag precision</th><td>{ci(r.metrics.missing_flag_precision)}</td></tr>}
          </>}
          <tr><th>Blocking findings</th><td>{r.blocking}{r.extractor === 'fixture' ? ' (all from the must-fail case, as intended)' : ''}</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Drug mismatches</th><td>{r.drug_mismatch}</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Unsourced items</th><td>{r.unsourced}{r.extractor === 'fixture' ? ' (the must-fail case)' : ''}</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Missing-information recall</th><td>{pct(r.missing_recall)}</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Per case</th><td><details><summary>{r.per_case.filter((c: any) => c.pass).length} of {r.per_case.length} passed</summary>{r.per_case.map((c: any) => `${c.pass ? '✓' : '✗'} ${c.id}`).join(' · ')}</details></td></tr>
        </table>
      </div>
    ))}
    <p class="notice">On prepared records every rate is perfect by construction: these records are the answer key. The row shows that the checks and the counting work, not how good the model is. Only the model run below measures that.</p>
    {!model && <p class="notice warn">Model runs are not published yet. The test set has 98 synthetic cases written in-house (English, German, Italian, including shorthand, typos, contradictions, a second animal in the notes, horses, rabbits, ferrets and birds; 87 of them generated line by line from <code>app/src/cli/gen-cases.ts</code>) and the model run is pending a development key. Before any clinic uses the product, the set grows to at least 300 cases across English, German and Italian, written with practising vets, and an external vet panel reviews a sample of the output. Those results will appear here.</p>}

    <h2>A test your clinic can repeat: 20 cases with a stopwatch</h2>
    <p>Numbers from a vendor, ours included, are no substitute for trying it on your own notes. This protocol takes about two hours and needs no data to leave the clinic.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Pick 20 recent consultations, a mix of routine and complicated ones. Copy the notes as the vet wrote them and remove the owner's name and contact details.</li>
      <li>For each case, write the record the way you normally do and time it, from first keystroke to finished entry.</li>
      <li>On another day, paste the same notes into the workspace, resolve every flag and approve. Time that too, from paste to approval.</li>
      <li>For every approved record, count: facts that are not in the notes (should be 0), facts from the notes that are missing, flags that were wrong, and edits you had to make.</li>
      <li>Report the median time per case for both methods, the four counts, and anything you would not sign. Send it to us if you like; we publish clinic results only with permission, named or anonymous as you choose.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Known gaps, honestly</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The test set is synthetic and written in-house. Real notes are messier, and the planned 300 cases written with practising vets are not there yet.</li>
      <li>Number checks are strict by design: a dose written as "5mg" and recorded as "5 mg" passes, but "five" written in words does not count as a number in the notes, so the record must keep "five". We prefer a false alarm to a silent invention.</li>
      <li>Drug-name matching tolerates typos by prefix; unusual brand names may need the vet's correction, which is then recorded as a human edit.</li>
      <li>Some errors the automatic checks cannot see yet, so the vet's reading is what catches them: a contradiction in the notes resolved silently in one direction, and a fact about another animal that the notes mention. A wrong status is now checked when the item's own text states one clearly: declined shown as done is blocked, planned shown as done and an owner's report shown as the vet's finding are flagged. A status the text does not state, or a line that mixes several, still relies on the vet's reading. The test set contains cases for each of these, so we can measure progress.</li>
      <li>No independent evaluation has taken place yet.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The test harness, the rules and the cases are in the project repository, so a reviewer can rerun them.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
